          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between flu and a cold?",
          "answer": "**Flu and the common cold are both viral infections of the nose and throat, but they usually feel different.**\n\n- **Onset:** Flu often starts suddenly, and people can often name the hour it began. A cold usually builds up gradually. (Influenza guidance; upper respiratory infection summary)\n- **Symptoms:** Flu typically brings fever, chills, muscle aches, headache, marked tiredness and a dry cough. A cold is mostly nasal symptoms and sore throat, and rarely causes high fever or severe exhaustion in adults. (Both summaries) These patterns only shift the odds. They cannot tell you for certain which illness someone has.\n- **Severity:** Flu is more severe than a cold and can lead to serious complications, especially in older adults, young children, pregnant people and people with long-term conditions such as asthma, COPD, heart failure or diabetes. A cold is self-limiting in most healthy people. (Influenza summary; upper respiratory infection summary)\n\n**When to seek care:** The retrieved sources do not give a full list of warning signs for flu, so I can't set out all the criteria here. One pattern they do describe is a worsening after several days of getting better, which is worth reporting to a clinician. If you have trouble breathing, chest pain, confusion, or feel very unwell, seek urgent care straight away. If you are in a higher-risk group, ask a clinician early. The sources also say antiviral treatment works best when started within 48 hours of symptoms starting, so it is worth raising with a clinician quickly. Whether it is right for you is a decision for them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
